Chapter: EmojiStillsSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter EmojiStills
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 EmojiStills: eight baked animal tables, selected by Unicode codepoint.

 THE DISPATCH IS BY CODEPOINT AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT WORTH GRADING. Where
 CatStills selects a pose by a small index, this chapter selects an animal by
 numbers like 129414 and 128024 -- six digits apart in ways no reader checks by
 eye, and adjacent in ways that matter: giraffe is 129426 and zebra is 129427.
 A transposed digit or an off-by-one lands on a real animal and draws the wrong
 one, silently, forever. All eight are graded and so is the miss.

 THE BULL IS THE ONLY ANIMAL WITH GRADIENTS AND THAT IS ASSERTED, not observed.
 Forty of its forty-three polygons carry a StillGrad; every other table in this
 port is solid. The chapter's own prose says the bull has a stop at alpha zero --
 a gradient fading to nothing rather than to a colour -- so it is the single
 exerciser of the shaded path in the whole renderer. If a regenerated table lost
 those gradients the bull would still draw, as a flat shape, and nothing else
 would complain.

 THE VALUES ARE CAPTURED AND SAY SO, as in CatStillsSpec: bake_stills.py
 generates this from the art and the oracle for the drawing is the blitter diff.
 What this file asks is whether the chapter compiles, loads, dispatches, and
 still holds what it held.

 WHAT COSTS HERE IS NAMING A TABLE, NOT WALKING ONE. A nullary binding emits as
 a FUNCTION (PORTING_NOTES B13), so every mention of `bull-polys` rebuilds all
 2,309 of its points; measured on the cat, the cost is exactly linear in
 references and nothing is memoised. So each table is reached ONCE, through
 `emoji-polys-for` -- which is the dispatch this file exists to grade -- and
 everything worth knowing about it is computed from that one reference.

 We say:

Section: Counting

 Everything worth knowing about one table, from a SINGLE reference to it.

  pts-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  pts-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.pts + pts-in ps (i + 1)

  grads-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  grads-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.grad + grads-in ps (i + 1)

 The first polygon's colour comes along for the ride: duck, elephant, rhino and
 pig all have six polygons, so no count separates them and the colour is the
 cheapest thing that does. An empty table has no first polygon, so the fallback
 arms report a colour of zero rather than reading one.

  facts : List StillPoly -> List Integer
  facts (ps) =
    if list-length ps == 0 then [0, 0, 0, 0]
    else [list-length ps, pts-in ps 0, grads-in ps 0, (list-at ps 0).color]

  emoji-facts : Integer -> List Integer
  emoji-facts (cp) = facts (emoji-polys-for cp)

Section: The Eight Animals, Through The Dispatch

 In chapter order: duck, elephant, giraffe, zebra, rhino, bull, cow, pig. Then
 two misses -- 129425 sits between giraffe at 129426 and rhino at 129423 and
 belongs to neither, which is the miss a fat-fingered codepoint would actually
 produce; 0 is the trivial one.

 THE BULL CARRIES FORTY GRADIENTS AND NOTHING ELSE CARRIES ANY. It is the only
 shaded table in the port and so the only exerciser of the renderer's shaded
 path; if a regenerated table lost those, the bull would still draw, as a flat
 shape, and nothing else would complain.

  table-got : List Integer
  table-got = (emoji-facts 129414) & (emoji-facts 128024) & (emoji-facts 129426) & (emoji-facts 129427) & (emoji-facts 129423) & (emoji-facts 128002) & (emoji-facts 128004) & (emoji-facts 128022) & (emoji-facts 129425) & (emoji-facts 0)

  table-want : List Integer
  table-want =
    [ 6, 189, 0, 4493595,
      6, 257, 0, 6513507,
      24, 850, 0, 16745005,
      22, 713, 0, 10197915,
      6, 237, 0, 13882323,
      43, 2309, 40, 4989733,
      16, 469, 0, 10197915,
      6, 243, 0, 16735833,
      0, 0, 0, 0,
      0, 0, 0, 0 ]

Section: The Shaded Path

 The bull's first gradient, read through the at-most-one list: its kind and its
 two offsets. Kind 1 is linear, 2 is radial. This is the one place in the suite
 that reaches INTO a StillGrad that came off the baker rather than one this
 suite constructed, which is what StillsSpec does instead.

  bull-grad : StillGrad
  bull-grad = list-at (list-at bull-polys 0).grad 0

  bg-int-got : List Integer
  bg-int-got = [bull-grad.kind]

  bg-int-want : List Integer
  bg-int-want = [1]

  bg-real-got : List Real
  bg-real-got = [bull-grad.off0, bull-grad.off1]

  bg-real-want : List Real
  bg-real-want = [0.0, 1.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-table" table-got table-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-bgi  " bg-int-got bg-int-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "es-bgr  " bg-real-got bg-real-want 0.0)
  end
